    "judgment": {
      "status": "confirmed",
      "reasoning": "All 16 answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. q-73484: only III is closed and lies entirely where cos(x) is nonnegative. q-73481: the fourth root requires x≥0, so II and III qualify. q-73495: I contains a jump at zero and III is not closed; only II qualifies. q-131066: I contains a jump at zero and III contains the undefined tangent value at π/2; only II qualifies. q-29664: the solutions in degrees are approximately −53.130102 and 126.869898, whose product rounds to −6741. q-120646: the equation reduces to cot(x)=0, giving 90° and 270°, with product 24300. q-62069: the symmetric prism net gives 2(24)+2(45)+54=192 cm². q-62107: 2(4·1+4·1+1·1)=18 m². q-120078: the symmetric prism net gives 2(36)+2(72)+108=324 cm². q-37900: continuity makes the left-hand limit equal to f(−1)=−3. q-104148: both one-sided limits equal 2, whereas the function value is 3. q-15641: the inverse derivative is the reciprocal of −2/3, namely −3/2. q-75987: f⁻¹(4)=−3/2 and f′(−3/2)=4, so the inverse derivative is 1/4. q-37070: cancellation gives x/2 for x≠3/4, producing a removable hole and no vertical asymptote. The source's wording about a denominator never being zero applies to the reduced denominator 2; the original denominator does vanish at 3/4, which the source subsequently acknowledges. q-73031: neither denominator factor cancels, giving vertical asymptotes at x=1 and x=−1. q-28273: conjugate roots give (x+1)²+3=x²+2x+4; multiplying by 4 gives the required equation. No earlier submitted answers or grades were supplied; confirmation rests on the captured worked solutions.",
